  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 8 — COMMON MISTAKES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="omm-section omm-section-tinted" aria-labelledby="omm-mistakes-heading">
    <h2 id="omm-mistakes-heading" class="omm-section-h2">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <div class="omm-mistake">
      <h3 class="omm-mistake-h3">Assuming Original Medicare Alone Covers All Hospital Costs</h3>
      <p class="omm-mistake-body">Many seniors enroll in Medicare Part A and Part B and assume they are fully covered. In reality, Original Medicare pays 80% of Part B services with no annual cap on the 20% you owe. A single hospitalization can expose you to thousands in uncapped coinsurance. Medigap exists specifically to close this gap. Without it, a serious illness can generate bills of $10,000 or more in a single year.</p>
      <p class="omm-mistake-fix">The fix is to enroll in a Medigap policy — ideally Plan G — during your 6-month Open Enrollment window when no medical underwriting is required. Find your window dates at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/supplements-other-insurance/when-can-i-buy-medigap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — When Can I Buy Medigap</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="omm-mistake">
      <h3 class="omm-mistake-h3">Not Accounting for IRMAA When Budgeting for Retirement Healthcare</h3>
      <p class="omm-mistake-body">IRMAA surcharges can add $974.40 to $5,844 per year to a couple's Medicare costs in 2026. Most retirement planners and financial advisors omit IRMAA from early retirement healthcare budgets. Failing to plan for it surprises many new retirees — especially those who had a high income year due to a Roth conversion, property sale, or large IRA withdrawal.</p>
      <p class="omm-mistake-fix">The fix is to use our <a href="/tools/medicare-irmaa-calculator/" class="omm-link">Medicare IRMAA calculator to estimate your surcharge</a> before large income events. IRMAA uses a two-year lookback, so 2024 income sets your 2026 surcharges. Verify your bracket at <a href="https://secure.ssa.gov/poms.nsf/lnx/0601101020" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSA.gov — Medicare Premium Determination</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="omm-mistake">
      <h3 class="omm-mistake-h3">Choosing Plan N Without Understanding the Real Copay Exposure</h3>
      <p class="omm-mistake-body">Seniors often choose Plan N for the lower monthly premium without realizing that the $283 annual Part B deductible, $20 per office visit, and $50 per ER visit (up to 26 visits per year for some seniors) can eliminate most of the premium savings. For a senior with eight annual doctor visits and one ER visit, Plan N's copay exposure alone is $210/year — on top of the $283 deductible.</p>
      <p class="omm-mistake-fix">The fix is to calculate your expected visit count and compare the real net cost of Plan N versus Plan G using the adjustable visit fields in this tool. Review Plan N's official benefit rules at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/supplements-other-insurance/whats-covered-by-medigap-policies" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — What Medigap Covers</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="omm-mistake">
      <h3 class="omm-mistake-h3">Keeping the Same Part D Plan Year After Year Without Comparing</h3>
      <p class="omm-mistake-body">Part D plans change their drug formularies, tiers, and premiums every January. A plan that covered your medications at Tier 2 in 2025 may move them to Tier 3 or even remove them entirely in 2026. Seniors who stay in the same plan without annual comparison routinely overpay by $500–$2,000 per year on drug costs alone, according to Medicare Payment Advisory Commission (MedPAC) research.</p>
      <p class="omm-mistake-fix">The fix is to compare Part D plans using the Medicare Plan Finder every year during the Annual Enrollment Period (October 15 – December 7). Enter your actual drug list at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/plan-compare" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov/plan-compare — Medicare Plan Finder</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="omm-mistake">
      <h3 class="omm-mistake-h3">Missing the Medigap Open Enrollment Window and Losing Guaranteed Issue Rights</h3>
      <p class="omm-mistake-body">The Medigap Open Enrollment Period is exactly six months long and begins only once — the month you are 65 and enrolled in Part B. If you delay enrolling in Part B (which some seniors do to stay on employer coverage), your Medigap window is also delayed. Missing it without a qualifying Special Enrollment Period means insurers in most states can charge you more or deny coverage entirely based on health history.</p>
      <p class="omm-mistake-fix">The fix is to confirm your Part B enrollment date and count forward six months — that is your window. Find your enrollment dates and rules at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — When Does Medicare Coverage Start</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


